WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.180
Every job needs the team.

2
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.040
But how much of a cell is each part?

3
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.240
You can't pour a cell into a measuring cup.

4
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:16.200
So how do scientists find out how much of a cell is mitochondria?

5
00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.610
First, they preserve the cell and cut a very thin slice.

6
00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:26.730
A transmission electron microscope sends a beam of electrons through the slice.

7
00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:31.030
It shows small organelles that a school microscope cannot.

8
00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:36.790
Because the cell was preserved, each picture is a still view, not a living cell.

9
00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:40.510
Next comes a simple trick called point counting.

10
00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.050
Lay an even grid of dots over the picture.

11
00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:46.890
Then look at where each dot lands.

12
00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:50.833
Dots that land on mitochondria turn gold.

13
00:00:52.113 --> 00:00:54.013
One slice gives only a rough guess.

14
00:00:55.293 --> 00:01:00.733
Over many fairly chosen pictures, the share of gold dots estimates the share of the

15
00:01:00.733 --> 00:01:02.993
cell's volume that mitochondria fill.

16
00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:05.567
Try it on a small grid.

17
00:01:06.947 --> 00:01:09.187
Count the gold dots, then count all the dots.

18
00:01:10.347 --> 00:01:11.747
The share appears on the screen.

19
00:01:13.127 --> 00:01:16.507
This toy grid lands inside the real range for heart muscle.

20
00:01:17.617 --> 00:01:21.017
Real studies used many pictures and many more dots.

21
00:01:22.417 --> 00:01:25.197
Look at heart muscle cells from ten kinds of mammals.

22
00:01:26.877 --> 00:01:31.377
Mitochondria filled about a fifth to over a third of the cell, depending on the kind

23
00:01:31.377 --> 00:01:32.377
of mammal.

24
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.460
Now drop the same grid on a white fat cell.

25
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.440
Before the dots turn gold, predict.

26
00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:42.360
Will its share be bigger or smaller than the heart cell's?

27
00:01:48.917 --> 00:01:53.217
Much smaller. Almost every dot lands on fat.

28
00:01:54.537 --> 00:02:00.817
One team laid a grid of 682 points over pictures of white fat cells from chicken

29
00:02:00.817 --> 00:02:05.317
embryos. Fat droplets filled about nine tenths of the cell.

30
00:02:06.757 --> 00:02:08.877
Mitochondria filled less than one percent.

31
00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:11.730
The fat takes most of the floor.

32
00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:17.370
Everything else, mitochondria included, sits in a thin rim around it.

33
00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.410
Is that rim short of mitochondria?

34
00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:23.350
The lesson lets you work it out.

35
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.600
So in the heart cell, mitochondria fill a large share.

36
00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.820
In the fat cell, stored fat fills most of the space.

37
00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.360
Many of the same parts, different floor plans.

38
00:02:36.433 --> 00:02:42.013
One caution. These cells came from different animals and different studies.

39
00:02:43.293 --> 00:02:45.933
They show a pattern across cells with different jobs.

40
00:02:47.173 --> 00:02:48.793
They do not prove what causes it.

41
00:02:49.733 --> 00:02:55.053
Still, simple counting, repeated over many pictures, turned images into numbers.

42
00:02:56.373 --> 00:03:01.153
Those numbers show a pattern: cells with different jobs have different floor plans.
